  "reasoning": {
    "household": "A single adult aged 35 to 40 with £12,000 of employment income in every year, and nothing else: no children, no savings, dividend or property income, no salary sacrifice, no fuel or rail spending, no rent or mortgage, and no disability or caring. The route's situation sets none of those (build_situation), and the request gives none.",
    "income tax and NI": "£12,000 (£11,980.80 in 2029 and 2030, that is 2029-30 and 2030-31, after the cap's haircut) is below the personal allowance (£12,570, or more where a measure's baseline uprates it) and below the employee National Insurance primary threshold (£12,570 a year in every year here, which the model applies as £241.73 a week, £12,570 / 52; the regulations' weekly threshold is £242; no measure changes it). So income tax and employee NI are £0 in every year. Asserted for the baseline each year.",
    "universal_credit": "Nil in every year, so no measure can move it. A single adult with no children, housing costs, disability or caring has no work allowance and no housing element, so the 55% taper applies to all of the month's earnings. That is £1,000 a month, so the taper takes £550; in 2029 and 2030 (2029-30 and 2030-31), while the salary sacrifice cap applies, the model's broad-base haircut (0.16%) leaves £998.40 a month and a taper of £549.12. Either exceeds the single 25-and-over standard allowance in every year: £400.14 a month in 2025-26 and £424.90 in 2026-27, the statutory rates, and policyengine-uk's projections of £434.67, £443.36, £452.23 and £461.28 for 2027 to 2030. Asserted for the baseline and for both sides of all eight measures, the salary sacrifice cap's included, so a later uplift that made this household eligible fails here instead of quietly changing what the fixture means.",
    "two_child_limit": "Changes only the UC and child tax credit child limit (child_count). The household has no children and no UC: £0.",
